program            = 010_comma_operator_sequencing
area               = 06_control_flow
description        = Comma operator sequencing with observable side effects in defined order, covering chains, nesting, precedence against assignment, argument position, for and while and do-while clauses, both conditional branches, sequenced volatile writes, and switch and if controlling expressions
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. The property under test is an
evaluation ORDER, so the observation must not be removable: the trace length is
held in a volatile counter and a volatile tick is incremented on every side
effect, which prevents dead-code elimination from eliding the appends. Measured
on the companion short-circuit program at -O2, the volatile form emits 74 counter
accesses against 34 for the plain form, confirming the qualifier is load-bearing.

No object is modified twice between sequence points. The expression that writes
the same volatile object three times is legal precisely because the comma
operator interposes a sequence point between each pair of operands, which fully
determines both the final stored value and the value of the whole expression.
Every other volatile access stands alone in its own full expression or is read
once into a plain local.

The trace array holds sixteen ints and every write goes through an explicit
range check, so no out-of-range write is ever performed; the longest trace in the
program is four entries, so the guard never fires but the bound is nevertheless
enforced. The array has static storage duration and is therefore zero
initialised, and reset_trace rewrites all sixteen elements before each traced
case, so no indeterminate storage is ever read. Every trace identifier is a
single decimal digit from 1 to 9, so the printed run of digits is unambiguous;
identifier 0 is never used because it could not be distinguished from an
unwritten slot.

All arithmetic is on int values of small magnitude, so no signed overflow is
possible. There are no shifts, no pointer arithmetic and no type punning,
therefore no aliasing violation and no dependence on padding bytes. The only
character data is a string literal label passed as const char *; no character
value is ever printed as a number. The for loop converges at i equal to j equal
to five, and the while and do-while loops are bounded by i less than three with
an increment in the body, so every loop terminates.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Nothing implementation-defined is observed. Only int values and one string label
are printed, so the measured plain-char signedness difference (signed on x86_64
and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) cannot affect the output, and the
measured width difference for long and pointers (4 bytes on i686, 8 elsewhere)
is never exposed because no long, pointer, size_t or ptrdiff_t value is printed
and no sizeof result appears in the output. No address is printed. No floating
point is used. Every ordering asserted here is fixed by the C sequence-point
rules rather than left to the implementation, so it is identical on every target.
All four targets and all three optimization levels are therefore compared, and
all three oracles are enabled.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
comma_chain order=123 len=3 tick=3
comma_chain_value=3
comma_nested order=1234 len=4 tick=4
comma_nested_value=4
comma_precedence order=12 len=2 tick=2
comma_precedence_value=1
comma_in_argument order=45 len=2 tick=2
comma_in_argument_value=5
for_comma i=5 j=5 iterations=5
while_comma_condition order=6666 len=4 tick=4
while_comma_condition_i=3
comma_in_ternary_true order=7 len=1 tick=1
comma_in_ternary_true_value=70
comma_in_ternary_false order=8 len=1 tick=1
comma_in_ternary_false_value=80
comma_volatile_writes r=2 vsel=2
comma_in_switch_control order=9 len=1 tick=1
comma_in_switch_control_value=20
comma_in_if_control order=12 len=2 tick=2
comma_in_if_control_value=2
comma_in_do_while order=555 len=3 tick=3
comma_in_do_while_i=3
END
